# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Source of scripts/content/rmo_rotation_matrices_euler_rpy_practical1.json.

TSI, M-407-01 «Robotics Modelling». Fifty questions on Sessions 1–3 of the
Moodle course (export 2026-11-20): L1 Position, Orientation and Rotation
Matrices, L2 Euler Angles and Roll-Pitch-Yaw, Practical 1 «Describing
Orientation with Rotation Matrices and RPY» and their worked solutions.

Notation follows the course:
  * {}^{A}p — point expressed in frame A; {}^{A}_{B}R — columns are B's axes in A;
  * R_x, R_y, R_z as on the slides (R_y has +sin top-right);
  * ZYZ proper Euler R = R_z(α) R_y(β) R_z(γ) (moving axes, post-multiply);
  * fixed-axis XYZ roll-pitch-yaw R = R_z(ψ) R_y(θ) R_x(φ) — roll φ, pitch θ,
    yaw ψ, the URDF convention (Lecture 2 letters). Practical 1 writes the same
    matrix as R_z(γ) R_y(β) R_x(α) with α = roll; questions that use the
    practical say «roll, pitch, yaw» in words to avoid clashing with ZYZ α, β, γ;
  * Practical 1 cell frame {0}: x0 down the aisle, y0 toward the conveyor,
    z0 up; body frame x forward (boresight), y left, z up.

Convention note. Lecture 2 / Self-check 2 calls R_x R_y R_z «extrinsic XYZ».
As a fixed-axis story that product applies R_z first, so it is intrinsic XYZ
= extrinsic ZYX; question 32 describes it in those exact terms and only uses
what the Vibe Lab verified (the known-case column).

Every number and formula is recomputed in
%TEMP%/lt-author-rmo_rotation_matrices_euler_rpy_practical1/verify.py (sympy).

Run:  python scripts/authoring/rmo_rotation_matrices_euler_rpy_practical1.py
Then: python scripts/check_quality.py rmo_rotation_matrices_euler_rpy_practical1 && python scripts/build.py
"""
import json
import os
import random

SLUG = "rmo_rotation_matrices_euler_rpy_practical1"
Q = []


def add(q, correct, wrong, explain, without, with_, benefit, code=False):
    assert len(wrong) == 3, q
    Q.append(dict(q=q, correct=correct, wrong=wrong, explain=explain,
                  usage=dict(code=code, without=without, with_=with_, benefit=benefit)))


